(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_morphologyoffloweringplants=[{id:"neet-2026r-q098",pn:98,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Phyllotaxy",q:"Phyllotaxy is the pattern of arrangement of ________.",o:["flowers","fruits","sepals","leaves"],c:3,s:"Phyllotaxy is the pattern of arrangement of leaves on the stem."},{id:"neet-2026-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Types of placentation",q:`Match List I with List II :
List I (Placentation) \u2014 List II (Example)
A. Marginal \u2014 I. Mustard
B. Axile \u2014 II. Pea
C. Parietal \u2014 III. Marigold
D. Basal \u2014 IV. Lemon`,o:["A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],c:2,s:`Marginal placentation is found in pea.
Axile placentation is found in lemon.
Parietal placentation is found in mustard.
Basal placentation is found in marigold.`},{id:"neet-2026-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Regions of the root",q:"In angiosperms, root hairs arise from which one of the following regions of the root?",o:["The root cap zone","The region of meristematic activity","The region of elongation","The region of maturation"],c:3,s:"In the region of maturation, some epidermal cells form very fine, delicate, thread-like structures called root hairs."},{id:"neet-2026-q112",pn:112,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Inflorescence types",q:"In racemose inflorescence, ____ .",o:["The main axis terminates in a flower","The growth is limited","Flowers are borne in an acropetal succession","Flowers are solitary"],c:2,s:`In racemose inflorescence the main axis keeps growing and the flowers are borne laterally in an acropetal succession.
In cymose inflorescence, by contrast, the main axis ends in a flower and so growth is limited.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q118",pn:118,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Placentation",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Marginal placentation \u2014 I. Argemone
B. Axile placentation \u2014 II. Tomato
C. Parietal placentation \u2014 III. Primrose
D. Free central placentation \u2014 IV. Pea
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],c:2,s:`Marginal placentation \u2013 Pea.
Axile placentation \u2013 Tomato.
Parietal placentation \u2013 Argemone.
Free central placentation \u2013 Primrose.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q119",pn:119,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Family Solanaceae",q:`Which of the following are characteristic features of Solanaceae family?
(a) Flowers are bisexual and actinomorphic
(b) Calyx have five sepals and are united
(c) Androecium have five stamens and are epipetalous
(d) Ovary is inferior
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(d) only","(a) and (b) only","(b), (c) and (d) only","(a), (b) and (c) only"],c:3,s:`In Solanaceae the flowers are bisexual and actinomorphic.
The calyx has five sepals that are united.
The androecium has five stamens and is epipetalous.
The ovary is superior, so (d) is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2026-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Family Solanaceae",q:"Which of the following is the correct floral formula of Solanaceae family?",o:["\u26A5 K\u208D\u2085\u208E C\u208D\u2085\u208E A\u2085 G\u0332\u208D\u2082\u208E (the arc joins C\u208D\u2085\u208E and A\u2085)","\u26A5 K\u2085 C\u208D\u2085\u208E A\u2085 G\u0332\u208D\u2082\u208E (the arc joins C\u208D\u2085\u208E and A\u2085)","\u2295 \u26A5 K\u208D\u2085\u208E C\u208D\u2085\u208E A\u2085 G\u0332\u208D\u2082\u208E","\u26A5 K\u2085 C\u2085 A\u2085 G\u0332\u208D\u2082\u208E"],c:0,s:`The formula \u26A5 K\u208D\u2085\u208E C\u208D\u2085\u208E A\u2085 G\u0332\u208D\u2082\u208E is the floral formula of Solanaceae.
It shows an actinomorphic, bisexual, pentamerous flower with epipetalous stamens.
In Solanaceae the calyx (K) and corolla (C) show fusion of sepals and petals respectively.
So the formula must show gamosepalous and gamopetalous conditions.`,fig:{w:230,h:140,alt:"Four floral formulas for Solanaceae, options 1 to 4.",d:[["t",4,30,"(1)","s"],["t",32,30,"\u26A5",""],["t",50,30,"K\u208D\u2085\u208E",""],["t",82,30,"C\u208D\u2085\u208E",""],["t",116,30,"A\u2085",""],["t",140,30,"G\u0332\u208D\u2082\u208E",""],["p","M88 19 Q108 6 122 19",""],["t",4,64,"(2)","s"],["t",32,64,"\u26A5",""],["t",50,64,"K\u2085",""],["t",82,64,"C\u208D\u2085\u208E",""],["t",116,64,"A\u2085",""],["t",140,64,"G\u0332\u208D\u2082\u208E",""],["p","M88 53 Q108 40 122 53",""],["t",4,98,"(3)","s"],["t",28,98,"\u2295",""],["t",60,98,"\u26A5",""],["t",78,98,"K\u208D\u2085\u208E",""],["t",110,98,"C\u208D\u2085\u208E",""],["t",144,98,"A\u2085",""],["t",168,98,"G\u0332\u208D\u2082\u208E",""],["t",4,132,"(4)","s"],["t",32,132,"\u26A5",""],["t",50,132,"K\u2085",""],["t",82,132,"C\u2085",""],["t",116,132,"A\u2085",""],["t",140,132,"G\u0332\u208D\u2082\u208E",""]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q104",pn:104,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Floral formula symbols",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: In a floral formula \u2295 stands for zygomorphic nature of the flower, and G\u0332 stands for inferior ovary.
Statement II: In a floral formula \u2295 stands for actinomorphic nature of the flower and G\u0332 stands for superior ovary.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],c:3,s:`The floral formula symbol \u2295 is used for an actinomorphic flower, while % is used for a zygomorphic flower.
The symbol G stands for gynoecium; underlined G (G\u0332) means superior ovary, and inferior ovary is shown by G with a bar above it (\u1E20).
So statement I is incorrect and statement II is correct.`,fig:{w:200,h:60,alt:"The floral formula symbol G with a line under it, and G with a bar over it.",d:[["t",40,38,"G","m"],["l",30,44,50,44],["t",150,38,"G","m"],["l",140,20,160,20]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Structure of seed",q:"In the seeds of cereals, the outer covering of endosperm separates the embryo by a protein-rich layer called :",o:["Coleoptile","Coleorhiza","Integument","Aleurone layer"],c:3,s:"In monocot seeds, the outer covering of endosperm separates the embryo by a proteinous layer called the aleurone layer."},{id:"neet-2025-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Seeds and embryo of monocot",q:`Match List I with List II :
A. Scutellum \u2014 I. Persistent nucellus
B. Non-albuminous seed \u2014 II. Cotyledon of Monocot seed
C. Epiblast \u2014 III. Groundnut
D. Perisperm \u2014 IV. Rudimentary cotyledon
Choose the option with all correct matches.`,o:["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],c:0,s:`Scutellum is the cotyledon of a monocot seed.
Groundnut seed is a non-albuminous seed.
Epiblast is the rudimentary cotyledon in a monocot seed.
Perisperm is persistent nucellus.`},{id:"neet-2025-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Flower symmetry",q:"Which of the following is an example of a zygomorphic flower?",o:["Petunia","Datura","Pea","Chilli"],c:2,s:`A zygomorphic flower can be divided into two equal halves by only one vertical plane. It shows bilateral symmetry.
Pea has zygomorphic flowers.
Chilli, Petunia and Datura have actinomorphic flowers.`},{id:"neet-2024-q104",pn:104,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Structure of dicot seed",q:"Identify the part of the seed from the given figure which is destined to form root when the seed germinates.",o:["C","D","A","B"],c:0,s:`The radicle is the part destined to form the root.
In the given diagram, C represents the radicle.`,fig:{w:150,h:160,alt:"Dicot seed cut open with four arrows A to D pointing to inner parts; C marks the radicle.",d:[["p","M30 12 C60 4 86 30 87 70 C88 110 80 140 55 148 C28 155 14 120 13 80 C12 45 18 18 30 12Z","B"],["p","M16 112 C40 100 66 82 86 62"],["p","M60 94 C60 80 70 68 83 68"],["p","M66 94 C66 84 72 76 82 75"],["p","M72 94 C72 88 76 82 82 81"],["p","M40 116 L52 140 L64 118"],["l",104,76,84,72,"a"],["l",104,92,76,88,"a"],["l",104,112,52,126,"a"],["l",104,142,56,141,"a"],["t",108,80,"A"],["t",108,96,"B"],["t",108,116,"C"],["t",108,146,"D"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q116",pn:116,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Position of floral parts (perigynous)",q:"Identify the type of flowers based on the position of calyx, corolla and androecium with respect to the overy from the given figure (a) and (b)",o:["(a) Perigynonus; (b) Epigynous","(a) Perigynous; (b) Perigynous","(a) Epigynous; (b) Hypogynous","(a) Hypogynous; (b) Epigynous"],c:1,s:`If the gynoecium sits in the centre and the other floral parts lie on the rim of the thalamus at almost the same level, the flower is perigynous.
Both diagrams show the perigynous condition.`,fig:{w:260,h:164,alt:"Two flower sections (a) and (b), each with the ovary in a cup-shaped thalamus and sepals, petals and stamens on the rim at the level of the ovary.",d:[["p","M30 100 C34 128 46 138 60 138 C74 138 86 128 90 100 C80 108 70 112 60 112 C50 112 40 108 30 100Z","B"],["p","M50 112 C46 92 48 76 60 70 C72 76 74 92 70 112Z","f"],["l",60,70,60,30],["p","M54 30 C54 22 66 22 66 30","B"],["p","M30 100 C14 92 8 70 12 40 C20 20 40 8 46 24 C40 40 34 70 34 94Z","f"],["p","M90 100 C106 92 112 70 108 40 C100 20 80 8 74 24 C80 40 86 70 86 94Z","f"],["l",38,96,36,50],["l",82,96,84,50],["c",36,46,4,"F"],["c",84,46,4,"F"],["l",44,110,52,138],["l",76,110,68,138],["t",28,156,"(a)","s"],["p","M160 100 C164 128 176 138 190 138 C204 138 216 128 220 100 C210 108 200 112 190 112 C180 112 170 108 160 100Z","B"],["p","M180 112 C176 92 178 76 190 70 C202 76 204 92 200 112Z","f"],["l",190,70,190,30],["p","M184 30 C184 22 196 22 196 30","B"],["p","M160 100 C144 92 138 70 142 40 C150 20 170 8 176 24 C170 40 164 70 164 94Z","f"],["p","M220 100 C236 92 242 70 238 40 C230 20 210 8 204 24 C210 40 216 70 216 94Z","f"],["l",168,96,166,50],["l",212,96,214,50],["c",166,46,4,"F"],["c",214,46,4,"F"],["l",174,110,182,138],["l",206,110,198,138],["t",158,156,"(b)","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Symmetry of flower",q:"Which of the following is an example of actinomorphic flower?",o:["Pisum","Sesbania","Datura","Cassia"],c:2,s:`Datura has actinomorphic (radially symmetrical) flowers.
Pisum, Sesbania and Cassia have zygomorphic flowers.`},{id:"neet-2024-q147",pn:147,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Flower and fruit features",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List I \u2014 List II
A. Rose \u2014 I. Twisted aestivation
B. Pea \u2014 II. Perigynous flower
C. Cotton \u2014 III. Drupe
D. Mango \u2014 IV. Marginal placentation
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],c:2,s:`Rose has a half-inferior ovary, so its flower is perigynous.
In pea, the placenta forms a ridge along the ventral suture of the ovary, with ovules in two rows (marginal placentation).
Cotton has twisted aestivation.
Mango fruit is a drupe.
So A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III.`},{id:"neet-2024-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Androecium types",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List I (Types of stamen) \u2014 List II (Example)
A. Monoadelphous \u2014 I. Citrus
B. Diadelphous \u2014 II. Pea
C. Polyadelphous \u2014 III. Lily
D. Epiphyllous \u2014 IV. China-rose
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],c:2,s:`China rose has a monoadelphous androecium.
Pea has a diadelphous androecium.
Citrus has a polyadelphous androecium.
Lily has an epiphyllous androecium.
So A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III.`},{id:"neet-2023-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Placentation types",q:"Axile placentation is observed in -",o:["China rose, Petunia and Lemon","Mustard, Cucumber and Primrose","China rose, Beans and Lupin","Tomato, Dianthus and Pea"],c:0,s:`Axile placentation is seen in China rose, Tomato, Petunia and Lemon.
Dianthus and Primrose have free central placentation.
Pea, Lupin and Beans have marginal placentation.
Cucumber and Mustard have parietal placentation.`},{id:"neet-2023-q121",pn:121,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Families: Fabaceae, Solanaceae, Liliaceae",q:"Family Fabaceae differs from Solanaceae and Liliaceae. With respect to the stamens, pick out the characteristics specific to family Fabaceae but not found in Solanaceae or Liliaceae.",o:["Epiphyllous and Dithecous anthers","Diadelphous and Dithecous anthers","Polyadelphous and epipetalous stamens","Monoadelphous and Monothecous anthers"],c:1,s:`Fabaceae has diadelphous stamens and dithecous anthers.
Solanaceae has free stamens (polyandrous), epipetalous, with dithecous anthers.
Liliaceae has free stamens (polyandrous), epiphyllous, with dithecous anthers.
So diadelphous stamens are specific to Fabaceae.`},{id:"neet-2023-q147",pn:147,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Flower as modified shoot",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :
Assertion A : A flower is defined as modified shoot wherein the shoot apical meristem changes to floral meristem.
Reason R : Internode of the shoot gets condensed to produce different floral appendages laterally at successive node instead of leaves.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],c:1,s:`A flower is a modified shoot whose apical meristem becomes a floral meristem.
The internodes do not elongate, so the axis is condensed.
The apex gives floral appendages at successive nodes instead of leaves.
So both are true and R explains A.`},{id:"neet-2022-q111",pn:111,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Modifications of stem and root",q:`Identify the correct set of statements :
(a) The leaflets are modified into pointed hard thorns in Citrus and Bougainvillea
(b) Axillary buds form slender and spirally coiled tendrils in cucumber and pumpkin
(c) Stem is flattened and fleshy in Opuntia and modified to perform the function of leaves
(d) Rhizophora shows vertically upward growing roots that help to get oxygen for respiration
(e) Subaerially growing stems in grasses and strawberry help in vegetative propagation
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a) and (d) Only","(b), (c), (d) and (e) Only","(a), (b), (d) and (e) Only","(b) and (c) Only"],c:1,s:`Thorns of Citrus and Bougainvillea come from axillary buds, not leaflets, so (a) is wrong.
Statements (b), (c), (d) and (e) are correct.`},{id:"neet-2022-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Fabaceae floral features",q:"Which one of the following plants shows vexillary aestivation and diadelphous stamens?",o:["Pisum sativum","Allium cepa","Solanum nigrum","Colchicum autumnale"],c:0,s:`Vexillary aestivation and diadelphous stamens are typical of family Fabaceae.
Pisum sativum (garden pea) is in Fabaceae.
Allium cepa (onion) and Colchicum autumnale are in Liliaceae.
Solanum nigrum is in Solanaceae.`},{id:"neet-2022-q134",pn:134,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Floral symmetry",q:`The flowers are Zygomorphic in:
(a) Mustard (b) Gulmohar (c) Cassia (d) Datura (e) Chilly
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(b), (c) Only","(d), (e) Only","(c), (d), (e) Only","(a), (b), (c) Only"],c:0,s:`A zygomorphic flower can be cut into two similar halves in only one vertical plane.
Examples: pea, gulmohar, bean, Cassia.
Mustard, Datura and chilli have actinomorphic flowers.`}]})();
